---
// The hero is the only display-scale element on the page (BRIEF §5).
//
// The two lines are CONTENT, not layout (BRIEF §4/§9): they must render as two
// distinct lines with a visible beat between them at EVERY breakpoint, and the
// second line is set in a lighter tone. They are separate block elements so they
// can never collapse into one flowing paragraph.
//
// FONT: the hero is set in Fraunces (self-hosted static instance at
// opsz 144 · wght 400 · SOFT 0 · WONK 0) via --font-serif. Its axes are baked
// into the instance, so no font-variation-settings are needed here.
---

<h1 class="hero">
  <span class="hero__line hero__line--lead">The good part is happening off-screen.</span>
  <span class="hero__line hero__line--soft">I build apps that know it.</span>
</h1>

<style>
  .hero {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: clamp(2.45rem, 1.1rem + 6.1vw, 5.25rem);
    line-height: 1.08;
    letter-spacing: -0.011em;
    margin-top: clamp(2.75rem, 8vw, 5.5rem);
    /* The single deliberate grid exception: the hero fills the full page width,
       running wider than the content measure. No max-width cap. */
    max-width: none;
    text-wrap: balance;
  }

  .hero__line {
    display: block;
  }

  .hero__line--lead {
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  /* the beat between the two lines + the lighter second tone (BRIEF §4) */
  .hero__line--soft {
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    margin-top: 0.32em;
  }
</style>
